We can use the identity $\binom{n}{k}=\binom{n-1}{k}+\binom{n-1}{k-1}$ to compute $\binom{15}{7}$. 
\begin{align*}
\binom{15}{7} &= \binom{14}{7} + \binom{14}{6} \\
&= 3432 + \binom{14}{7} \\
&= 3432 + \binom{13}{7} \\
&= 3432 + \binom{13}{7} + \binom{13}{6} \\
&= 3432 + \binom{13}{7} + \binom{13}{6} + \binom{13}{6} \\
&= 3432 + 2\binom{13}{6} + \binom{13}{6} \\
&= 3432 + 2\binom{13}{6} + \binom{13}{6} \\
&= 3432 + 3\binom{13}{6}
\end{align*}We are given that $\binom{14}{8} = 3003$ and $\binom{14}{7} = 3432$. Thus, we have:
\begin{align*}
\binom{15}{7} &= 3432 + 3\binom{13}{6} \\
&= 3432 + 3(3003) \\
&= 3432 + 9009 \\
&= \boxed{12441}
\end{align*}

